\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The real commutant and multiplicity matrices}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Let a finite group #{G} act orthogonally on a finite-dimensional
real space #{H}. In its [isotypic decomposition](fdip-004V), write
#{H_\Xi\cong W_\Xi^{\oplus m_\Xi}}, where #{W_\Xi} is irreducible
and #{m_\Xi>0} is its \newvocab{multiplicity}. The
\vocabk{commutant}{fdip-003T} consists of all linear maps commuting
with #{G}. With multiplication given by composition, put
#{D_\Xi=\operatorname{End}_G(W_\Xi)}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
D_\Xi&\cong\mathbb R,\ \mathbb C\ \text{or}\ \mathbb H,\\
\operatorname{End}_G(H)&\cong\prod_\Xi M_{m_\Xi}(D_\Xi).
\end{aligned}}
Let #{M_\Xi=\operatorname{Hom}_G(W_\Xi,H_\Xi)} be the
\vocabk{right multiplicity module}{fdip-00KN}, with
#{D_\Xi}-valued pairing #{h(f,g)=f^*\circ g}. Postcomposition makes
the commutant act on #{M_\Xi} on the left. A right #{D_\Xi}-basis of
#{M_\Xi} identifies that action with left matrix multiplication, and
the isomorphism can be chosen to carry adjunction to conjugate
transpose. If #{H=\mathbb R^V} is a vertex permutation representation,
the real dimension of #{\operatorname{End}_G(H)} is the number of
orbits of the diagonal action
#{g(u,v)=(gu,gv)} on #{V\times V}. These orbits are called
\newvocab{orbitals}.}

\proof{
\p{The adjoint of #{f\circ d} is #{d^*\circ f^*}, so
#{h(f\cdot d,g\cdot e)=d^*\circ h(f,g)\circ e}. Orthogonality gives
#{b_H(f(gw),v)=b_W(w,g^{-1}f^*v)}, hence #{h(f,g)} intertwines and
lies in #{D_\Xi}. Maschke's theorem decomposes #{H} orthogonally into irreducibles.
The kernel and image of an intertwiner are invariant. Hence an
intertwiner between inequivalent irreducibles is zero, and every
nonzero endomorphism of one irreducible is invertible. Thus
#{D_\Xi} is a finite-dimensional associative real division algebra;
Frobenius's division-algebra theorem gives the three displayed
possibilities. Choose a right #{D_\Xi}-basis of the multiplicity module, and
identify its #{m_\Xi} elements with isometric copies of #{W_\Xi}
by the normalization below. Between those copies the intertwiners
are precisely matrices of elements of #{D_\Xi}, acting by
#{(Aw)_i=\sum_j A_{ij}(w_j)}. The product order is the order of
composition. This proves the algebra statement. The
[basis form](fdip-00KO) records conjugate transpose and the orbital
indicator basis.
The real classification and its representation-theoretic use are
\citet{Section 13.2, pp. 106-109}{serre1977linear}; compare the
[real-type distinction](fgap-001Y).}

\p{Adjunction preserves #{D_\Xi}. A self-adjoint element of it has
real eigenspaces invariant under #{G}, so irreducibility makes it a
real scalar. In particular #{d^*d=cI} with #{c>0} for #{d\ne0}.
The resulting positive adjoint on #{\mathbb R}, #{\mathbb C} or
#{\mathbb H} is the usual conjugation: for an imaginary unit
#{j^2=-1}, write #{j+j^*=2aI} with #{a\in\mathbb R}.
Then #{j^*j=2aj+I} is scalar, forcing #{a=0}.
Thus #{j^*=-j}.
Identifications between equivalent orthogonal copies can be made
isometric by dividing an intertwiner by the positive square root
of its scalar #{T^*T}. In those coordinates the block adjoint is
conjugate transpose.}

\p{For a vertex permutation representation, use the vertex indicator
basis. A matrix #{A} in that basis commutes with every #{\rho(g)}
exactly when #{A_{gu,gv}=A_{u,v}}. Its entries are therefore constant
on orbitals. The indicator matrices of distinct orbitals have disjoint
supports and form a real basis of the commutant, proving the dimension
formula.}
}

\p{The scalar-side convention matters for #{\mathbb H}.
Here #{D_\Xi} acts on #{W_\Xi} on the left by endomorphisms.
If instead #{W_\Xi} is presented as a left module for a simple
matrix algebra, its commuting scalars act on the right and form
an opposite algebra, as in \ref{fcap-0042}. Quaternion conjugation
identifies that opposite algebra with #{\mathbb H}; it does not
license reversing the order of quaternion products.}
